<p>The Ritual Chamber is a circle. Perfect, mathematically exact, as if drawn by a compass the size of the room. The walls are white stone — the same luminous material as the tower's exterior — curved inward to meet a domed ceiling painted with stars. Not decorative stars. Enchanted ones. They move — slow, stately orbits that mirror the actual sky above, as if the dome is transparent and I'm looking through it at the heavens. The effect is disorienting and beautiful. I'm inside and outside simultaneously. I'm standing in a room and standing under an infinite sky.</p>

<p>The altar sits at the center. Low, wide, carved from a single block of grey stone. Runes circle its surface — spiralling patterns that pulse with a faint silver light, rhythmic as a heartbeat. The stone is draped in silvery cloth — thin, almost translucent, shimmering where the candlelight touches it. Hundreds of candles ring the room — white wax in iron holders, their flames steady and unflickering. Enchanted. Nothing in this room moves unless the magic wills it.</p>

<p>The five sorceresses take their positions — Ilara at the head of the altar, the other four at the cardinal points. The air pressure increases. My ears pop. The hum I've felt since entering the tower intensifies into a vibration I can feel in my teeth, my bones, the wet tissue behind my eyes.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"Undress." Her voice is calm and absolute. The voice of a woman who has done this before — perhaps not the Rite itself, but rituals of this magnitude. She is not nervous. She is precise.</div>

<p>I hesitate. Standing in a room of five women, about to strip bare, knowing that the body I reveal is the last time it will exist in this form — the hesitation is not modesty. It's grief. Undressing feels like uncovering a corpse.</p>

<p>I remove the sword belt first. Set it against the wall. The weight leaves my hip and I feel unbalanced, tilted, as if the sword was the counterweight that kept me upright.</p>

<p>The jerkin. The laces give easily — cross, loop, pull, the last time these fingers do this in this order with these hands. I set it beside the sword. The leather is warm where it touched my body. It retains my shape for a moment, then collapses.</p>

<p>The shirt. Over my head. The air hits my bare chest — cool, charged, prickling with magic. My chest. Flat. Muscled. The ladder of my ribs visible when I inhale. The trail of dark hair from navel to waistband. The scar on my left ribcage from the sparring accident at sixteen. All of it. Mine. For the last time.</p>

<p>Trousers. Boots. Smallclothes. Each garment removed is a layer peeled from the person I am, and by the time I'm naked — standing bare in the silver-white light of enchanted candles and enchanted stars — I feel not exposed but excavated. Stripped to the foundation. The raw material of a human body, offered up for demolition and reconstruction.</p>

