<p>Ilara leans over me. Her face is framed by the spinning stars. Her expression is carefully neutral, but her eyes are soft — the professional compassion of a healer about to cause necessary pain.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"Drink."</div>

<p>A goblet. Silver, ancient, warm despite the chamber's cool. The potion inside shifts between colours — silver, deep violet, a blue so dark it's almost black. It catches the candlelight and refracts it into tiny rainbows on the stone walls. It smells like moonlight — I don't know how else to describe it. Cold and silver and distant and clean.</p>

<p>I drink.</p>

<p>It tastes like iron and flowers and something I have no word for — a flavour that bypasses the tongue and registers directly in the blood, in the marrow, in the chromosomes themselves. Like swallowing a key that opens every lock in the body simultaneously. I drink it all. I don't sip. Vael said not to sip.</p>

<p>Warmth. Spreading from my stomach outward, through my chest, down my limbs, into my fingers and toes. Not unpleasant. Like drinking hot wine on a cold night, if the wine were liquid starlight and the night were the last night of your life.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ILARA</span>"The Rite of Becoming begins." Her voice, formal, resonant, filling the chamber. "May the Mother accept this offering and return it transformed."</div>

<p>The chanting starts. Five voices in unison — low, resonant, in a language older than the kingdom. I don't understand the words. I don't need to. The words aren't for me. They're for the magic, which listens to them the way a horse listens to reins — understanding not the meaning but the intent, the direction, the command.</p>

<p>The runes on the altar flare. Silver-white, blinding. I close my eyes and see the light through my eyelids — red-orange, pulsing. The candles dim. The painted stars begin to spin faster.</p>

<p>Then the pain begins.</p>

<hr>

